WOW-BG3 bg3le patch 0013: where the game's buffers live in host memory, and its descriptor sets' dynamic bindings, on every driver (on top of 0001-0012).

A native plugin that reads the game's buffers on the CPU (WOW-BG3's render plugin: the per-view camera block, effect
data) needs each buffer's memory, offset and the memory's mapping. An early plugin (patch 0005) starts at the game's
first pipeline cache or swapchain, after the game made, bound and mapped its constant rings. bg3le is on the game's
path from its first vkGetDeviceProcAddr, so it keeps the record: vkCreateBuffer, vkDestroyBuffer, vkBindBufferMemory(2),
vkMapMemory, vkUnmapMemory and vkFreeMemory are answered with recorders (always on), and
bg3le_vk_buffer_host(buffer, &size) gives a buffer's bytes while its memory is mapped over it. Before this the render
plugin read RADV's own objects: no layout fitted on Nvidia's driver, and the world was not drawn there.
The same for descriptor sets: vkCreateDescriptorSetLayout, vkAllocateDescriptorSets and their undoing are recorded, and
bg3le_vk_set_dynamic(set, ...) gives a set's dynamic bindings (binding, descriptor count) in binding order: the plugin
counted them from the slots it saw written, which missed the game's early sets and templates (7 offsets read as 5:
BG3's state rebound with those lost the device on Nvidia).

